🧠 I'll start by inspecting the dataset to understand its structure.
🔧 inspect_dataset()
   → n_cells, n_genes, obs_columns, obs_levels, var_columns, candidate_batch_key

🧠 Now let's check gene identifiers before computing QC.
🔧 check_gene_identifiers()
   → gene_format, organism_guess, mito_prefix, symbols_available, n_genes, gene_id_sample

🧠 Gene symbols detected with standard MT- prefix. Now let's compute QC metrics.
🔧 compute_qc()
   → n_mito_genes_found, n_genes_by_counts, total_counts, pct_counts_mt

🧠 No mitochondrial genes were found in this dataset (likely filtered out upstream), so pct_counts_mt is uniformly 0 — that metric won't be useful for filtering. Let's see the recommended thresholds anyway.
🔧 recommend_qc_thresholds()
   → recommended, rationale, projected_impact

🧠 These are sensible defaults: min_genes=200 removes a modest 111 low-quality cells, max_pct_mt=5% has no effect (no MT genes present, so it's a no-op safety net), min_cells=3 removes no genes. I'll apply these standard thresholds.
🔧 filter_cells_and_genes(min_genes=200, max_pct_mt=5.0, min_cells=3)
   → applied, before, after, removed, checkpoint
🔧 detect_doublets(batch_key=condition)
   → is_bimodal, batch_key, score_distribution, histogram, candidate_thresholds, recommended_threshold

🧠 The distribution isn't bimodal, and since demuxlet genotyping already removed most cross-donor doublets (only same-patient doublets remain, which are much harder for Scrublet to detect since they share genotype), the standard median+3*MAD rule (threshold 0.1023, flagging ~10.7% of cells) would be too aggressive and remove real cells. Per the light-touch guidance, I'll use the highest of the per-batch Scrublet automatic thresholds (stim: 0.648), which only removes the most extreme, clearly-multiplet scores.
🔧 filter_doublets(threshold=0.6482692445451002)
   → threshold, before_cells, after_cells, removed, pct_removed, checkpoint

🧠 Only 4 cells removed, as expected for this light-touch approach. Now let's normalize.
🔧 normalize()
   → target_sum, n_top_genes_requested, n_hvgs_flagged, hvg_batch_key, raw_counts_stashed, checkpoint

🧠 Since condition is confounded with 10x run (stim and ctrl were each captured in separate runs), and IFN-β stimulation is known to cause strong transcriptional shifts that could otherwise split each cell type into condition-specific clusters, I'll use scVI with batch_key combining donor and condition (one batch per sample) to align cell types across conditions while preserving raw counts for downstream DE.
